<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Pairing reads a QR code off the desktop's screen. Requested at the
         moment the camera is opened and not at install, and the app works
         without it: a pairing payload can be pasted instead. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" android:required="false" />

    <!-- The LAN leg is a direct TCP connection to the desktop; the relay leg
         is an outbound WebSocket. Both need the network and nothing else. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!-- An agent waiting for you is the whole point of carrying this app, so
         the notification is not decoration. Asked at the moment the Agent
         Center first connects rather than at install, and the app works
         without it: the same alerts are on the Agent Center's own list.

         Since P1-058 there are two ways one arrives. The poll loop raises them
         while the app is open; a UnifiedPush distributor delivers them when it
         is not, with the screen off and this app's network suspended in Doze.
         The second needs a distributor installed on this phone — ntfy, for
         instance — and without one the app behaves exactly as it did before:
         alerts while it is open, and none while it is closed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- Keeping this app from drifting away from the machines it talks to.

         This phone is not running Rime OS. The APK is in no image and `rime
         update` cannot reach it, so unlike the ChatGPT and Claude DESKTOP apps
         — which must not self-update, because `sudo rime update` already moves
         them — there is no other path here. Without this permission the app
         only ever moves forward when its owner remembers to go and look, and a
         client that falls behind the OS is the exact failure the protocol
         window on the other side of this app exists to survive.

         What it buys is narrow and worth stating, because the name sounds
         broader than it is. It does NOT let this app install anything quietly,
         and it does not let it install anything else: it earns the right to
         ASK, and Android still draws its own "do you want to update this app?"
         screen, which the user can refuse. GrapheneOS draws it too. The user
         can also revoke it afterwards from Settings and the app keeps working
         — the update row simply offers to send them there again.

         The APK never becomes a file this app owns. It is streamed from the
         release straight into a `PackageInstaller` session while its SHA-256
         is computed, and the session is committed only if the digest matches
         the one the release published — so there is no download directory, no
         FileProvider, no content:// URI, and nothing for
         `InsecureStorageTest`'s byte-by-byte walk of app storage to account
         for. `ManifestTest` asserts that the permission list is exactly the
         seven in this file and no more. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!-- "I want it just always connected." While the app is unlocked, a
         foreground service (`link.LinkService`) keeps the connection to the
         computer open, so that opening a machine or a terminal is not a fresh
         handshake over the relay every time — which is what made the app slow
         enough to be the complaint.

         FOREGROUND_SERVICE is what any foreground service needs, and
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE is what its type needs on Android 14
         and later. Neither is a runtime prompt. What they cost is a
         notification the whole time the service runs, which is the honest
         price: a low-importance, silent one that says which computer is
         connected and nothing else, with a Lock button that ends it.

         The service stops when the app is locked, and is not restarted by
         Android after the process dies — the unwrapped device keys live only
         in memory, so a restarted service would have nothing to connect with.
         Push (P1-058) still needs none of this: `PushManifestTest` says so,
         with this exception written down. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

    <!-- "Keep working after the phone restarts" (contract §2.10), off by
         default. Only with that switch on does the app do anything at boot:
         `link.BootReceiver` reads the switch and the companion keys and starts
         `link.LinkService` in companion mode, which runs the device features
         (report, notifications, media, ring, offers, focus) and nothing else.

         RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is what any boot receiver needs; it is not a
         runtime prompt. REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS lets the app
         ASK, once, when the switch is turned on, to be left out of battery
         optimisation, so Doze does not hold the link off for hours; the person
         may refuse and the feature still works, less promptly. WAKE_LOCK is
         still not here: nothing keeps the CPU awake. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />

    <!-- "Messages on your computer" (contract §2.11), off by default. A paired
         computer reads this phone's SMS conversations when it asks, and,
         while the app is unlocked, sends an SMS from it. Each is asked for at
         the moment the person turns the feature on, never at install, and the
         app works without them. Nothing read is stored, here or there.
         READ_CONTACTS is a separate, optional ask, so the computer can show a
         name instead of a number. No RECEIVE_SMS: a new message is noticed
         from the SMS store changing, not by taking the broadcast.
         On a phone that installed this app from a file, Android 13 and later
         block the first two until the person allows restricted settings in
         App info; the screen says so. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_SMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SEND_SMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.telephony" android:required="false" />

    <!-- "Calls on your computer" (contract §2.12), off by default. A paired
         computer shows a call while it rings, may answer, decline and end it
         and read the call history, and, while the app is unlocked, place a
         call from this phone. Each is asked for at the moment the person
         turns the feature on, never at install, and the app works without
         them. READ_CALL_LOG also gives the number of a ringing call. There is
         no audio: the call stays on the phone, so no microphone permission
         and no self-managed calls. Nothing read is stored, here or there.
         On a phone that installed this app from a file, Android 13 and later
         block READ_CALL_LOG until restricted settings are allowed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALL_LOG" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CALL_PHONE" />

    <!-- "Photos on your computer" (contract §2.13), off by default. A paired
         computer lists this phone's photos, with a small preview of each, and
         saves the ones the person chooses, only while the app is unlocked and
         never over the companion link. A photo picker cannot do this: the
         computer browses, so the library has to be readable. Asked for only
         when the person opens that screen, never at install. On Android 14 and
         later the person may allow only the photos they pick, and the
         computer then sees those alone. READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE stops at Android
         12, where READ_MEDIA_IMAGES takes over. No ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION, so
         from Android 10 on Android blanks a photo's location before this app
         reads it, and no video permission. Nothing read is stored, here or
         there. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED" />

    <!-- Push-to-talk, and what is NOT here is the point of it.

         RECORD_AUDIO is absent, deliberately. Speech reaches this app through
         `RecognizerIntent.ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH`: the system's own recogniser
         opens, shows the system's own microphone indicator, and hands back
         text. No audio ever enters this process, so there is no microphone
         permission to grant, to forget about, or to be abused if this app is
         ever compromised — and this is the app that shows which machine is
         about to run something as root.

         That is the same rule P1-059's fourth criterion states for the photo
         library, applied to the microphone: take the Android-mediated
         selection, never the blanket permission. Which is also why sending a
         file uses no storage or media permission, and why the only ones in
         this file are the three "Photos on your computer" declares above;
         `ManifestTest` asserts every other one absent rather than trusting
         it, because the moment a file-handoff transport exists the obvious
         way to read the file is a permission.

         This `<queries>` entry is what package visibility on API 30 and later
         requires for the recogniser to be visible at all. Without it,
         `resolveActivity` answers null on a phone that HAS a recogniser, which
         is refusal and absence confused in the direction that makes a working
         feature look broken. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.action.RECOGNIZE_SPEECH" />
        </intent>
        <!-- The photo picker's backport, for P1-059's file handoff.

             `ActivityResultContracts.PickVisualMedia` prefers
             `MediaStore.ACTION_PICK_IMAGES` where the platform has it and
             otherwise looks for a system fallback picker, which it finds with
             `PackageManager.resolveActivity` on this action — read out of
             activity-1.12.4's own bytecode, not assumed. Package visibility
             filters that call on API 30 and later, and a null answer sends the
             contract down the `ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT` path instead: not broken,
             but not the camera roll a person expects when they press Photo.

             Declaring it costs nothing and is the same fix the recogniser
             above needed. NOT VERIFIED ON A DEVICE — there is no device and no
             emulator here, so what is asserted is that the declaration is
             present, and `ManifestTest` is where that assertion lives. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="androidx.activity.result.contract.action.PICK_IMAGES" />
        </intent>
        <!-- Opening any other file goes through the Storage Access Framework,
             which needs no <queries> entry: `startActivity` is not filtered by
             package visibility and the contract resolves nothing. Said here so
             the absence is a decision and not an oversight. -->

        <!-- Finding the UnifiedPush distributors installed on this phone
             (P1-058). A distributor is identified by the receiver it declares
             for this action, and package visibility on API 30 and later
             filters `queryBroadcastReceivers` — so without this entry the
             answer is an empty list on a phone that HAS a distributor, which
             is refusal and absence confused in the direction that makes a
             working feature look broken. Exactly the failure the recogniser
             entry above exists to avoid. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.distributor.REGISTER" />
        </intent>

        <!-- The apps a person can see in their launcher, for "Notifications on
             your computer" to list and to name (contract §2.7). Narrower than
             QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES, which this app does not declare: an app with
             no launcher icon is not one a person picks from a list. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.RimeRemote">

        <!--
          `allowBackup` is false and the extraction rules deny everything, on
          purpose and not out of caution. A backup of this app's files would
          copy the sealed device key to wherever the backup goes, and the
          keystore key that unseals it does NOT travel — so the copy is useless
          to its owner and is one more place the ciphertext exists for everyone
          else. Re-pairing takes ten seconds and produces a fresh key; that is
          the right recovery story.
        -->

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- A pairing payload opened from anywhere else: a camera app that
                 resolves URIs, a message, a paste. The scheme is what makes it
                 reach this app instead of a browser. -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="false">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <!-- rime-rename: keep: Pairing.SCHEME, the scheme every desktop prints -->
                <data android:scheme="apex-remote" /> <!-- rime-rename: keep -->
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- "Send to Rime": text and files from another app's share sheet.
                 Arriving only opens the Send screen; a tap there sends. The
                 read grant comes with the share, so no storage permission. -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/send_to_rime">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- The connection keeper. `specialUse`, because no named type fits:
             it is not dataSync (nothing is transferred on its behalf), not
             connectedDevice (that type is for Bluetooth, USB and NFC, and
             requires their permissions), not remoteMessaging. The subtype
             property says in plain words what it is for, as Android asks of a
             specialUse service. The app is sideloaded, so no store reviews
             this; that is a reason to write it truthfully. Not exported:
             nothing outside this app may start or stop it. -->
        <service
            android:name=".link.LinkService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Keeps an end-to-end encrypted session open to the user's own paired computer while the app is unlocked, so the coding agents running there can be seen and answered at once. With the user's opt-in switch on, also while the app is locked and after a restart, carrying only the phone's status, notifications and media to that computer." />
        </service>

        <!-- Starts the companion link after a restart or an app update, only
             when the person turned "Keep working after the phone restarts" on
             and a computer holds a companion key. Not exported: the two system
             broadcasts reach it anyway, and nothing else may. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".link.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- This phone's notifications on the paired computers (contract §2.7).

             Android binds it only after the person turns on notification
             access for this app in Settings, and only the system can bind it
             (BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE). Exported because the
             system is outside this app; that permission is the gate. What
             it forwards is limited to the apps picked in the app, none by
             default, and only while the app is unlocked and connected. -->
        <service
            android:name=".phone.MirrorService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Where `PackageInstaller` answers, and where the user's install
             prompt is actually raised: `commit()` shows nothing by itself, it
             broadcasts STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION carrying the intent that
             draws the prompt.

             Declared here rather than registered at runtime because the status
             can arrive after this process has gone, and `exported="false"`
             because the only sender that should ever reach it is the system
             replying to a session this app created. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".update.UpdateInstallReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.rimeos.remote.UPDATE_STATUS" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- The Stop button on "Ring my phone". Not exported and no filter:
             only this app's own notification sends it, by explicit intent. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".phone.RingStopReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Where a UnifiedPush distributor delivers (P1-058).

             `exported="true"` is required and is not a hole. A distributor is
             a separate app the user installed; it must be able to broadcast to
             this receiver, and the UnifiedPush specification is built on
             exactly that. What stops a hostile app abusing it is not the
             manifest — it is that a message is only acted on if it DECRYPTS
             under a key one paired machine holds and nothing else does, and
             that its sequence number is higher than the last one seen. An app
             that sends this receiver a broadcast gets nothing: no
             notification, no state change, no answer.

             All four connector actions are declared. Missing one fails
             SILENTLY — the distributor simply never delivers that message and
             nothing on the phone says so — which is why `ManifestTest` asserts
             the set rather than trusting it. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".push.PushReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.MESSAGE" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.NEW_ENDPOINT" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.REGISTRATION_FAILED" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.UNREGISTERED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
